{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Jethro, Moses' father-in-law, took.</b> Meaning: because he was Moses' father-in-law, he found it possible to do thus — to go to the mountain of Elohim, etc. — for they left him alone out of fear of Moses, since his dread had fallen upon all the peoples. But apart from this, they would have prevented him from going to free-think against their faith, all the more so since he was suspect in their eyes. And you will find that every nation imposes the death penalty upon one who rejects their faith, and it was only because Jethro was Moses' father-in-law that this stood in his favor. Or perhaps it further alludes that although Jethro had a title of greatness in being called 'priest of Midian,' he rejected this title and took for himself the designation 'Moses' father-in-law.' And that is what the verse says: <b>And Jethro took</b> — and what did he take? Of the two designations, the verse relates to him at the outset as <b>Moses' father-in-law</b>, not 'priest of Midian.'" "<b>After she had been sent away</b> — the reason it was necessary to say 'after she had been sent away,' and also the precision of using the language of 'sending away': apart from the words of our Sages, of blessed memory (Shemot Rabbah 4:4), which I have written in connection with the verse 'et all that He had done,' etc., it appears that Moses had sent her away when he saw that he was occupied with the mission of his commission, blessed be He; he did not know the measure of time and sent her away from his house. And the sons — Eliezer was young and needed his mother, for he was born on the road at the inn, and Gershom, presumably, was young, close in age to Eliezer before him. Or according to their words, of blessed memory, who said (Mekhilta) that he stipulated with Jethro: one son for the daughter and a second son for the father — for this reason both were in her hand. And the intention of HaShem's making the matter known here is to declare the praise of Jethro and the greatness of Moses in his eyes, for although he had sent her away, he took her and exerted himself with his honor and in person to bring her to him even after she had been sent away. But according to their words, of blessed memory (Shemot Rabbah), who said that he did not send her away but relied on Jethro's promise that he would bring her, it appears that the reason it says <b>after she had been sent away</b> means that the matter did not take place until she first sent messengers to Moses and knew the location of his stations and agreed through her agency — then it was that Jethro took, etc., and came to the wilderness, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The Hebrew phrase לכפור באמונתם literally means 'to heresy against their faith,' i.e., to abandon or reject their belief system. Read here as 'to free-think against their faith.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:29:29.926947102Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}